data.content:
Here's the complete migration. This code uses two patterns that don't exist in modern .NET:

1. **`Response.End()`** — internally calls `Thread.Abort`, which throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` in .NET 6+
2. **`Response.Redirect(url, true)`** — the `true` (endResponse) also calls `Thread.Abort`
3. **`catch (ThreadAbortException)`** — no longer needed; remove entirely

## Migrated ASP.NET Core Controller

```csharp
[ApiController]
[Route("[controller]")]
public class ReportController : ControllerBase
{
    private readonly ILogger<ReportController> _logger;

    public ReportController(ILogger<ReportController> logger)
    {
        _logger = logger;
    }

    [HttpPost("export")]
    public IActionResult Export()
    {
        // Response.Clear / Response.Write / Response.End are gone.
        // Return a FileContentResult instead — the framework handles
        // headers, content-type, and flushing.
        var data = GenerateReport();
        var bytes = System.Text.Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes(data);
        return File(bytes, "text/csv", "report.csv");
    }

    [HttpPost("redirect")]
    public IActionResult RedirectAction()
    {
        if (!User.Identity?.IsAuthenticated ?? true)
        {
            // Response.Redirect(url, true) → return RedirectResult.
            // No Thread.Abort, no ThreadAbortException.
            return Redirect("/login");
        }

        try
        {
            ProcessRequest();
            return Redirect("/success");
        }
        // ThreadAbortException catch block is removed entirely —
        // it cannot occur in modern .NET.
        catch (Exception ex)
        {
            _logger.LogError(ex, "Request processing failed");
            return Redirect("/error");
        }
    }

    private string GenerateReport() { /* ... */ return ""; }
    private void ProcessRequest() { /* ... */ }
}
```

## Key changes & pitfalls

| .NET Framework pattern | ASP.NET Core replacement | Pitfall to avoid |
|---|---|---|
| `Response.Write()` + `Response.End()` | Return `File(...)`, `Content(...)`, or `Ok(...)` | Don't call `HttpResponse.CompleteAsync()` as a substitute for `Response.End()` — just return an `IActionResult` |
| `Response.Redirect(url, true)` | `return Redirect(url)` | Don't pass `true` to any redirect — that parameter doesn't exist. You **must** `return` the result to stop execution |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | Remove the block entirely | Don't replace with `catch (OperationCanceledException)` here — that's only needed if you're doing long-running work with `CancellationToken` |
| `Response.Clear()` / `Response.AddHeader()` | Handled automatically by `IActionResult` | Don't manually set headers that `FileContentResult` already sets |
| `User.Identity.IsAuthenticated` | `User.Identity?.IsAuthenticated ?? true` | `Identity` can be `null` in ASP.NET Core — always null-check |

## Critical behavioral difference

In Web Forms, `Response.Redirect(url, true)` **aborted the thread** — code after it never ran. In ASP.NET Core, `Redirect()` returns an object. **If you forget the `return`, execution continues:**

```csharp
// BUG: ProcessRequest() still runs after Redirect!
Redirect("/login");
ProcessRequest(); // ← this WILL execute

// CORRECT: return stops execution
return Redirect("/login");
```

This is the single most common migration bug. Every `Response.Redirect` must become `return Redirect(...)`.

## For long-running exports, use `HttpContext.RequestAborted`

If `GenerateReport()` is slow, pass the cancellation token so the work stops if the client disconnects:

```csharp
[HttpPost("export")]
public async Task<IActionResult> Export(CancellationToken cancellationToken)
{
    // ASP.NET Core binds HttpContext.RequestAborted automatically
    var data = await GenerateReportAsync(cancellationToken);
    var bytes = System.Text.Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes(data);
    return File(bytes, "text/csv", "report.csv");
}
```
